### Why is a free-text status dangerous?

If status is a plain string that any service can write, nothing stops a bug or race from setting an order to an impossible value or sequence.

```java
package com.bank.order.antipattern;

public class FreeTextStatusWrong {

    private String status; // plain String

    // WRONG: any code can set any value
    public void setStatus(String newStatus) {
        this.status = newStatus;
    }

    // Bug: code accidentally sets Delivered -> Preparing
    // Result: restaurant starts preparing an already-delivered order
    //         customer is charged twice
    //         reporting shows impossible state
}
```

### How does a state machine prevent invalid transitions?

Define the complete set of states and a transition table listing which events are allowed from each state.

```java
package com.bank.order.statemachine;

import java.util.*;

public enum OrderState {
    CREATED, CONFIRMED, PREPARING, PICKED_UP, DELIVERED, CANCELLED, REFUNDED;
}

public enum OrderEvent {
    CONFIRM, START_PREPARING, PICK_UP, DELIVER, CANCEL, REFUND;
}

public class OrderStateMachine {

    private static final Map<OrderState, Map<OrderEvent, OrderState>> TRANSITIONS;

    static {
        TRANSITIONS = new EnumMap<>(OrderState.class);
        TRANSITIONS.put(OrderState.CREATED, Map.of(
            OrderEvent.CONFIRM, OrderState.CONFIRMED,
            OrderEvent.CANCEL, OrderState.CANCELLED));
        TRANSITIONS.put(OrderState.CONFIRMED, Map.of(
            OrderEvent.START_PREPARING, OrderState.PREPARING,
            OrderEvent.CANCEL, OrderState.CANCELLED));
        TRANSITIONS.put(OrderState.PREPARING, Map.of(
            OrderEvent.PICK_UP, OrderState.PICKED_UP,
            OrderEvent.CANCEL, OrderState.CANCELLED));
        TRANSITIONS.put(OrderState.PICKED_UP, Map.of(
            OrderEvent.DELIVER, OrderState.DELIVERED));
        TRANSITIONS.put(OrderState.DELIVERED, Map.of(
            OrderEvent.REFUND, OrderState.REFUNDED));
        // CANCELLED and REFUNDED: no transitions (terminal states)
        TRANSITIONS.put(OrderState.CANCELLED, Map.of());
        TRANSITIONS.put(OrderState.REFUNDED, Map.of());
    }

    private OrderState currentState;
    private int version = 0;

    public OrderStateMachine() {
        this.currentState = OrderState.CREATED;
    }

    public OrderState transition(OrderEvent event) {
        Map<OrderEvent, OrderState> allowed = TRANSITIONS.get(currentState);
        if (allowed == null || !allowed.containsKey(event)) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(
                "Invalid transition: " + currentState + " + " + event);
        }
        currentState = allowed.get(event);
        version++;
        return currentState;
    }

    public OrderState getCurrentState() { return currentState; }
    public int getVersion() { return version; }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        OrderStateMachine order = new OrderStateMachine();
        System.out.println("State: " + order.getCurrentState());
        order.transition(OrderEvent.CONFIRM);
        System.out.println("State: " + order.getCurrentState());
        order.transition(OrderEvent.START_PREPARING);
        System.out.println("State: " + order.getCurrentState());

        try {
            order.transition(OrderEvent.CONFIRM); // invalid!
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println("Rejected: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
State: CREATED
State: CONFIRMED
State: PREPARING
Rejected: Invalid transition: PREPARING + CONFIRM
```

### How does it pair with the Saga pattern?

Each Saga step is a state transition. Compensations are reverse transitions.

```java
package com.bank.order.saga;

public class OrderSagaWithStateMachine {

    private final OrderStateMachine stateMachine;

    public OrderSagaWithStateMachine(OrderStateMachine stateMachine) {
        this.stateMachine = stateMachine;
    }

    public void executeSaga() {
        // Step 1: Payment success -> CONFIRM
        stateMachine.transition(OrderEvent.CONFIRM);

        // Step 2: Restaurant accepts -> START_PREPARING
        boolean restaurantAccepted = callRestaurant();
        if (!restaurantAccepted) {
            // Compensation: cancel + refund
            stateMachine.transition(OrderEvent.CANCEL);
            refundPayment();
            return;
        }
        stateMachine.transition(OrderEvent.START_PREPARING);

        // The state always tells you exactly where the Saga is
        // and what compensation is needed.
    }

    private boolean callRestaurant() { return true; }
    private void refundPayment() { }
}
```

### How do idempotent transitions work?

Re-applying an event on an already-transitioned order is a safe no-op, not an error.

```java
package com.bank.order.statemachine;

public class IdempotentStateMachine {

    public OrderState transitionIdempotent(OrderState current, OrderEvent event,
                                            OrderState expectedTarget) {
        if (current == expectedTarget) {
            // Already in the target state: idempotent no-op
            return current;
        }
        // Normal transition
        return transition(current, event);
    }

    // Saga retries mark-delivered on a DELIVERED order -> no-op, safe.
}
```

### How does optimistic locking prevent concurrent corruption?

A version field ensures two concurrent transitions cannot both succeed.

```java
package com.bank.order.repository;

public class OrderRepository {

    public boolean updateState(String orderId, OrderState newState,
                                int expectedVersion) {
        // UPDATE orders SET state = ?, version = version + 1
        // WHERE order_id = ? AND version = ?
        // Affected rows = 0 means someone else transitioned first.
        int affected = executeUpdate(orderId, newState, expectedVersion);
        return affected > 0;
    }

    private int executeUpdate(String id, OrderState s, int v) { return 1; }
}
```

### How do you attach side effects to transitions?

Actions fire on entering a state, keeping behavior consistent with state.

```java
package com.bank.order.statemachine;

import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.Consumer;

public class TransitionActions {

    private static final Map<OrderState, Consumer<String>> ON_ENTER = Map.of(
        OrderState.CONFIRMED, orderId -> sendConfirmationSms(orderId),
        OrderState.CANCELLED, orderId -> issueRefund(orderId),
        OrderState.DELIVERED, orderId -> sendDeliveryReceipt(orderId)
    );

    public static void fireOnEnter(OrderState state, String orderId) {
        Consumer<String> action = ON_ENTER.get(state);
        if (action != null) action.accept(orderId);
    }

    private static void sendConfirmationSms(String id) { }
    private static void issueRefund(String id) { }
    private static void sendDeliveryReceipt(String id) { }
}
```

### How does idempotent order creation work?

A client-generated idempotency key ensures retries never duplicate.

```java
package com.bank.order.service;

public class IdempotentOrderCreation {

    public Order create(String customerId, String idempotencyKey) {
        Order existing = repository.findByIdempotencyKey(idempotencyKey);
        if (existing != null) return existing; // no duplicate

        Order order = new Order(customerId, idempotencyKey);
        repository.save(order);
        return order;
    }
}
```

